ethercat-host for Debian
------------------------

Configuration ownership (U3): this package ships NO /etc/ethercat.conf.
The EtherCAT master configuration is host-specific and is rendered by
the Ansible role (ethercat_master, cycle M7). The service fails closed
until a configuration is present: ethercatctl exits non-zero when
/etc/ethercat.conf is not readable, so `systemctl start ethercat`
fails rather than starting a master with no device.

Operator step for package-only hosts (no Ansible): copy the reference
example and edit it, then start the service:

    sudo cp /usr/share/doc/ethercat-host/examples/ethercat.conf /etc/ethercat.conf
    sudoedit /etc/ethercat.conf      # set MASTER0_DEVICE (and UPDOWN_INTERFACES)
    sudo systemctl enable --now ethercat.service

Group lifecycle (U4): the ethercat system group is declared via
sysusers.d, so it exists before the udev rule loads (closes the device
node group-ownership hazard). On purge the system group is retained per
Debian convention; this is reported as a note, not residue.

